data: {"event": "reasoning.available", "run_id": "run_6770cd2acb4c4de79b1d90840b826869", "timestamp": 1789921956.286238, "text": "The two most common reasons a local HTTP server returns connection refused are:\n\n1. **The server process died** \u2014 the process bound the port but exited, so nothing listens on it.\n2. **The port is actually closed** \u2014 the binding happened but then the socket was never released/closed, so the OS doesn't accept new connections.\n\nThe first command to run is:\n\n```bash\nss -tlnp | grep <port>\n```\n\nThis shows which processes have a listening socket on the given port. If nothing is listening, the server h"}

data: {"event": "run.completed", "run_id": "run_6770cd2acb4c4de79b1d90840b826869", "timestamp": 1789921956.2981918, "output": "The two most common reasons a local HTTP server returns connection refused are:\n\n1. **The server process died** \u2014 the process bound the port but exited, so nothing listens on it.\n2. **The port is actually closed** \u2014 the binding happened but then the socket was never released/closed, so the OS doesn't accept new connections.\n\nThe first command to run is:\n\n```bash\nss -tlnp | grep <port>\n```\n\nThis shows which processes have a listening socket on the given port. If nothing is listening, the server has died; if the port is open but nothing shows, the process likely crashed after binding.\n\nThen verify the process is alive:\n\n```bash\npgrep -f <server-lang-or-command>\n```\n\nIf you want, I can also check the full socket state with `lsof -i:<port>` or inspect `/proc` for the process holding the port.", "usage": {"input_tokens": 14407, "output_tokens": 193, "total_tokens": 14600}}

: stream closed

